{% extends "base.html.j2" %}
{% block title %}About the data{% endblock %}
{% block content %}
      <h1>About the data</h1>
      <h2>Sources</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Itemized receipts (Schedule A), independent expenditures (Schedule E) and committee
          history: the FEC's <a href="https://www.fec.gov/data/browse-data/?tab=bulk-data">PostgreSQL
          data dumps</a>, refreshed weekly by the FEC and here monthly.</li>
        <li>Candidates, committees and the links between them: FEC bulk files (candidate master,
          committee master, candidate-committee linkage, committee contributions to candidates).</li>
        <li>State map: US Census Bureau
          <a href="https://www.census.gov/geographies/mapping-files/time-series/geo/cartographic-boundary.html">cartographic
          boundary files</a> (public domain), as packaged by
          <a href="https://github.com/topojson/us-atlas">us-atlas</a>.</li>
      </ul>
      <h2>How the numbers are built</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>"For" money is what was given to a candidate's own committees, plus independent
          expenditures reported as supporting them. "Against" is independent expenditures
          reported as opposing them.</li>
        <li>Individual donors are matched by name and ZIP code, because the FEC does not assign
          individuals an ID. Two people with the same name in the same ZIP code may be combined.</li>
        <li>Only contributions over $200 are itemized with a donor name; smaller gifts appear only
          as a total per candidate.</li>
        <li>Memo entries are left out so that earmarked gifts are not counted twice.</li>
      </ul>
      <h2>Charts</h2>
      <p>Every chart uses both colour and a fill pattern (stripes, dots, cross-hatching) for each
        series, and lists every value in text, so it can be read without telling colours apart.</p>
{% endblock %}
